{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}

{% block title %}How ranking works - extdir{% endblock %}
{% block canonical %}<link rel="canonical" href="{{ url('ranking') }}">{% endblock %}
{% block meta_description %}The formula extdir uses to order extensions, rendered from the code that runs it.{% endblock %}

{% block body %}
<div class="wrap page">
    <div class="page-head">
        <h1>How ranking works</h1>
        <p>Read from the constants that do the ranking, so it cannot fall behind the code.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose">
        <h2>The formula</h2>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0">
            <table class="data">
                <thead>
                <tr><th>Factor</th><th>Weight</th><th>What it measures</th></tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                {% for label, row in weights %}
                    <tr>
                        <td><strong>{{ label }}</strong></td>
                        <td class="num">
                            {{ (row[0] * 100)|round }}%
                            {# The bar makes the shape of the formula visible; the
                               number stays because a weight is a fact, not a vibe. #}
                            <span class="weight-bar" style="width: {{ (row[0] * 100)|round }}%" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                        </td>
                        <td class="muted">{{ row[1] }}</td>
                    </tr>
                {% endfor %}
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2>What is not in it</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Popularity.</strong> An agency extension may have a dozen stars while an
            abandoned experiment has hundreds. Stars and installs are shown on <a href="{{
            path('boards') }}">the boards</a> and never feed the score.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Manual placement.</strong> No paid slots, no featured section, no per-vendor
            adjustment. A test fails the build if vendor-specific logic enters the ranking path.
        </p>

        <h2>Confidence in a compatibility claim</h2>
        <p>
            Support for the current release {% if currentVersion %}(<strong>{{
            currentVersion.majorMinor }}</strong>){% endif %} is graded:
        </p>
        <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0">
            <table class="data">
                <thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>Looks like</th><th>Multiplier</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
                <tbody>
                <tr>
                    <td>explicit</td><td><code>~6.6.0</code>, <code>&gt;=6.5 &lt;6.7</code></td>
                    <td class="num">1.0</td>
                    <td class="muted">Bounded inside one major.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>caret</td><td><code>^6.5</code>, <code>~6.6</code></td>
                    <td class="num">0.85</td>
                    <td class="muted">Expands to <code>&lt;7.0</code>, claiming releases published after the last commit.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>wildcard</td><td><code>*</code>, <code>&gt;=6.0</code></td>
                    <td class="num">0.4</td>
                    <td class="muted">Parses, but asserts nothing useful.</td>
                </tr>
                <tr>
                    <td>absent</td><td>no constraint</td>
                    <td class="num">0.2</td>
                    <td class="muted">Nothing declared on any Shopware package.</td>
                </tr>
                </tbody>
            </table>
        </div>

        <h2>Maintenance</h2>
        <p>
            Measured against Shopware's release dates, not the calendar. What breaks a finished
            extension is a new Shopware release.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Current</strong>: committed to since the newest Shopware minor shipped.</li>
            <li><strong>Lagging</strong>: quiet since then, active after the previous minor.</li>
            <li><strong>Dormant</strong>: silent across two or more minors <em>and</em> idle over two years.</li>
            <li><strong>Abandoned</strong>: the maintainer marked it abandoned on Packagist.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
            The two-year floor exists because Shopware minors have shipped five months apart.
        </p>

        <h2>Breaking ties</h2>
        <p>
            Every factor above is categorical, so healthy extensions bunch at the top. Time since
            the last commit decays over {{ recencyHalfLife }} days to separate them.
        </p>

        <h2>Changing the formula</h2>
        <p>
            The weights live in one class in the public repository. Any change benefiting the
            maintainer's own extensions needs a written rationale in the commit.
        </p>
    </div>
</div>
{% endblock %}
